# Who gets asked to review what. Later rules win, so the catch-all comes first.
#
# Two owners, because one is not enough to satisfy branch protection: GitHub
# never lets a PR author approve their own PR, so with a single owner
# "Require review from Code Owners" deadlocks every PR. @MrDClaudeBot is the
# second owner and approves what @mrdoodles raises.
#
# A CODEOWNERS entry only counts if that account has **write** access to the
# repository — an owner with read access is silently ignored and shows up in
# the repo's CODEOWNERS errors, which is why write access is granted before
# protection is turned on. Check with:
#   gh api repos/KanRite/rite/codeowners/errors

*                          @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot

# The library both binaries share (ADR-0004: "one library, two interfaces").
# A change here reaches the CLI and the desktop board at once — the whole
# point of the split, and the whole reason a mistake here is expensive.
/crates/rite-lib/           @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot

# The ticket file format: frontmatter shape, the yaml filter, the strict
# MiniJinja environment. rite-ui (the companion app, a separate repository)
# is a format consumer bound by whatever this produces.
/crates/rite-lib/templates/ @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot
/crates/rite-lib/src/task.rs        @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot
/crates/rite-lib/src/templates.rs   @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot

# tests/test.sh is the compatibility contract with v3 boards, per CLAUDE.md.
# A change here can silently narrow what the spec actually guarantees.
/tests/test.sh             @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot

# Release-critical. bin-name and the packaging shape decide whether a tagged
# release produces a working zip at all — see the known rust-release gap
# recorded in CLAUDE.md.
/.github/workflows/        @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot
/install.sh                @mrdoodles @MrDClaudeBot
